Ways the change could fail a criterion (what each rating is measured against):

  min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens  (reused from an earlier run)
    [boundary_wrong_side] min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens/exact-512-opening-not-marked
      The comparison in mark_reusable_opening (not shown in the diff, but it consumes _minimum_reusable_opening) may be strict (> minimum) rather than inclusive. An opening of exactly 512 tokens would then get no cache breakpoint on Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 or Haiku 5.5.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#3
      pairs: 12 put to the model, 251 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens/sonnet-5-5-resolved-as-sonnet-5
      Model-name resolution uses prefix matching against a table in which 'sonnet-5' (1024) and 'sonnet-5-5' (512) both match a Sonnet 5.5 name. If the longest-match rule fails, or the model is spelled in a form that does not start with the 'sonnet-5-5' key (for example 'claude-sonnet-5.5' with a dot), Sonnet 5.5 falls back to the Sonnet 5 minimum of 1024. Openings between 512 and 1023 tokens are then unmarked.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#3
      src/acceptance/llm.py#2
      pairs: 3 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens/table-minimum-wrong-for-a-5-5-model
      The per-model table could hold a value other than 512 for one of the three models, or lack its key. Opus 5.5 could fall through to the 'opus-5' or 'opus-4' families, and Haiku 5.5 to 'haiku-4-5' (4096) or the default. Any of these would give a minimum that differs from 512 for that model.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#2
      src/acceptance/llm.py#3
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 259 skipped once the defect was covered
    [not_wired] min-reusable-prefix-512-tokens/minimum-not-applied-to-stage-model
      The minimum might be looked up from the run-level model rather than the model the request is actually sent to, which can be a stage-specific override. The marking decision would then use another model's threshold for stages that run on a 5.5 model.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#3
      src/acceptance/llm.py#6
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 259 skipped once the defect was covered

  region-source-in-shared-opening
    [not_wired] region-source-in-shared-opening/regions-block-outside-reusable-opening
      The new REGIONS block kind (35) is added to the descriptor's messages, but `reusable_opening` may only treat certain block kinds (e.g. up to INSTRUCTIONS) as the reusable opening. If so, the region source falls after the cache breakpoint and is not shared, so each call repeats it.
      src/acceptance/request_blocks.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#1
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 259 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] region-source-in-shared-opening/region-text-differs-across-same-region-defects
      The REGIONS text is built from the regions offered to each defect, including the per-defect `region.label` in the header and the order of regions. Two defects implicating the same code can therefore render different text, for example with different labels or ordering, so the opening is not byte-identical and cannot be shared.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#1
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#3
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 259 skipped once the defect was covered
    [qualifier_ignored] region-source-in-shared-opening/region-source-still-repeated-in-subject
      The per-call SUBJECT may still contain region source text, so the per-call portion repeats it. Possible routes are the surrounding-context rendering (`render_contexts(contexts_for(region_spans(offered), surrounding))`) and the defect text.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#1
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#2
      pairs: 1 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered

  batch-api-opt-in
    [scope_too_narrow] batch-api-opt-in/batch-gate-excludes-routed-claude
      The decision to batch reuses `_needs_explicit_marker(request["model"])`, a prompt-caching predicate, as the test for 'is a Claude model'. Claude models reached under other model-name routes, or by a stage-specific model override, may fail that predicate and be sent directly even though the run has batch on.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#6
      pairs: 12 put to the model, 251 skipped once the defect was covered
    [qualifier_ignored] batch-api-opt-in/batch-requires-default-completion-fn
      Batching is applied only when the client's completion function is the default LiteLLM one. A run that enables batch while the client has a different completion function sends its Claude calls directly, with no indication that the opt-in was ignored.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#6
      pairs: 12 put to the model, 251 skipped once the defect was covered
    [not_wired] batch-api-opt-in/batcher-only-via-build-client
      The batcher reaches a client only through `RunConfig.build_client`, and it is passed to `ModelClient` only when `self.batch` is true. Any client built another way, such as a `client_factory` or a direct `ModelClient(...)` construction in other entry points, gets no batcher. Within `measure_instability`, `comparison_client` is also never given one. Those Claude calls then go direct despite the run's batch setting.
      src/acceptance/config.py#3
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#0
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#3
      pairs: 263 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] batch-api-opt-in/transport-ignored-by-litellm
      The batching depends on LiteLLM using an injected `client=` object for the Anthropic call. If LiteLLM ignores the client, or posts through a different path such as streaming, async or a different handler, the request goes to the direct `/v1/messages` endpoint and is never queued into a batch. The recording would still be marked `sent_via: batch`.
      src/acceptance/llm.py#6
      src/acceptance/llm.py#7
      src/acceptance/llm_batch.py#0
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 7 skipped once the defect was covered

  batch-setting-in-run-configuration
    [not_wired] batch-setting-in-run-configuration/client-factory-ignores-batch-setting
      In the instability benchmark, run_once sets batch on the RunConfig. When a client_factory is supplied, the client comes from the factory, which may not call RunConfig.build_client(). In that case the batch flag is carried in the config but never reaches the client, and calls go out directly.
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#0
      src/acceptance/config.py#3
      pairs: 13 put to the model, 250 skipped once the defect was covered
    [scope_too_narrow] batch-setting-in-run-configuration/batch-not-threaded-through-all-run-entrypoints
      The only caller shown passing the setting is the instability benchmark (run_once/measure_instability), whose default is batch=False. Other entry points that build a RunConfig, such as benchmark scoring and experiment scripts, are not changed to set or read it. The comparison_client and the perturbation draw may also get a config or client that lacks the setting.
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#0
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#1
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#3
      src/acceptance/config.py#2
      pairs: 263 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] batch-setting-in-run-configuration/batcher-shared-across-config-copies
      The batcher is held as a pydantic private attribute created lazily on the RunConfig. model_copy copies private state shallowly. If a config is copied after its batcher was created, the copy shares the same batcher, so two runs that should each hold their own state share one. If it is copied before, each copy gets a separate batcher, which breaks one-batcher-per-run sharing.
      src/acceptance/config.py#2
      src/acceptance/benchmark/instability.py#0
      pairs: 263 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered

  batch-api-opt-in-off-by-default
    none enumerated: Every path in the diff keeps batching off unless it is switched on. `RunConfig.batch` is declared `bool = False`. `build_client` passes `batcher=self._run_batcher() if self.batch else None`, so the guard is the right way round and a default config gets no batcher. `ModelClient.__init__` defaults `batcher` to None. `_live_call` only sets `batched` when `self._batcher is not None`, so with no batcher it adds no transport and no `sent_via`, and the call goes direct. `calls_in_flight` is None without a batcher, so `map_calls` keeps its default of eight. `run_once` and `measure_instability` default `batch=False` and only forward it into `RunConfig`. No code in the diff turns batching on implicitly. Because the off state is the declared default and every guard checks the same flag, there is no plausible static defect to enumerate.

