  [not_mutable] byte-identical-review-state-across-send-modes/batch-usage-and-cache-fields-differ
    candidate edits: 1 asked for, 0 set aside, none used
    pairs: 0 settled by the run, 4 put to the model, 0 dropped as not worth asking, 259 skipped once the defect was covered
    The defect is about how the provider serves a batched call. Batch cache hits are best-effort, so the cache read and creation token counts the provider returns can differ from a direct call. The code records whatever usage the reply carries, via _extract_usage(response), and does not choose or change those counts. A contiguous edit cannot make the recorded usage differ for this reason, because the difference comes from the provider and not from any line here. The only thing the code changes is cost_usd (halved by BATCH_PRICE_FACTOR), which is intentional pricing and not the cache-token behaviour the defect names. I also see no way to repair the code so that batched usage matches a direct call, because the direct values are not known to it.

  [already_present] byte-identical-review-state-across-send-modes/batched-cost-halved-in-record
    candidate edits: 1 asked for, 0 set aside, none used
    pairs: 0 settled by the run, 4 put to the model, 0 dropped as not worth asking, 259 skipped once the defect was covered
    Lines 1378-1379 of `_live_call` multiply `record["usage"]["cost_usd"]` by `BATCH_PRICE_FACTOR` only when `batched` is true. The direct path records LiteLLM's cost unchanged. I did not see the value of `BATCH_PRICE_FACTOR`; I am assuming it is 0.5, as the defect describes. The same request therefore records a different cost depending on send mode. The repair deletes the scaling so both modes record the same usage and cost.

